@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix bl: <https://dagalog.dev/ns/backlog#> .
@prefix crate: <https://dagalog.dev/ns/backlog/crate#> .
@prefix ghissues: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/issues/> .
@prefix ghpull: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/pull/> .

# Unlike invalid_orphan_issue.ttl (a fictional issue number, invented purely
# for a negative test), this is a REAL, currently-existing gap: issue #274
# genuinely has no parent epic (confirmed via the GitHub GraphQL API's
# `issue.parent` field returning null), despite this repo's own stated
# policy that every issue must be an epic or a sub-issue of one
# (CLAUDE.md, "Github backlog"). It is closed and resolved (PR #275) —
# the gap is structural (no epic link), not about the work itself being
# incomplete.
#
# Kept in its own file, separate from the purely-synthetic
# invalid_orphan_issue.ttl, so that file's own documented guarantee ("no
# real issue is broken") stays true, while still giving #285's SHACL shapes
# a second, real-world example of the same violation shape to catch —
# more convincing evidence the shapes work than a fictional case alone.
#
# Kept as-is (not fixed) — resolving it is a call for the repo owner, not a
# fixture-authoring decision. (Dag reviewing this: if you'd like #274 given
# a parent epic, that's a one-line `gh api .../sub_issues` call, independent
# of this backlog-mirror work.)

ghissues:274
    a bl:Issue, bl:WorkItem ;
    bl:number 274 ;
    rdfs:label "Apply configurable request body limit to RDF upload routes (/{name}/data, /rdf-graph-store) to avoid RequestEntityTooLarge" ;
    bl:state bl:Closed ;
    bl:hasLabel bl:Bug, bl:Ready .
    # No bl:subIssueOf — confirmed via GraphQL, not assumed.

ghpull:275 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    bl:number 275 ;
    rdfs:label "fix(sparql_endpoint): raise body-size limit on RDF write routes" ;
    bl:state bl:Closed ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:274 ;
    bl:touchesCrate crate:sparql_endpoint, crate:dagalog .
